title: Revert endpoints
tags: [[Revert]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: GET /revert/plan, the single-shot POST /revert, and the coordinated start/commit/abort — and the target they share.

|!Route |!Does |
|<<cmd "GET /repos/:repo/revert/plan">> `?target_rev_id=…` or `target_op_ids=…` |what a revert would do; writes nothing, takes no lock |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/revert">> |writes and commits in one transaction |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/revert/start">> |the coordinated form: takes the lock, fixes the target, answers the plan |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/revert/commit">> |`{"apply": [ids], "label"?}` — writes what the client managed, releases the lock |
|<<cmd "POST /repos/:repo/revert/abort">> |releases the lock, writes nothing → `{"head"}` |

''The target'', the same everywhere: `{"rev_id": N}`, `{"rev_id": "head"}`, or
`{"op_ids": [22, 23]}`; every member on HEAD's ancestry (`400` otherwise). Nothing
blocks a target: the revert is applied onto the current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]).

!! The plan

```
{"requires_lock": true,
 "operations": [{"id": 23, "op_type": "file_moved", "entity_uuid": "…", "field_name": "mfr_path",
                 "writes": "file_moved", "restores": [{"field_name": "mfr_path", "value_type": "tree_ref", …}],
                 "filesystem": {"action": "move", "from": "/repo/music/new.mp3", "to": "/repo/music/old.mp3"},
                 "changed_since": {"op_id": 91, "rev_id": 40, "timestamp": …, "op_type": "file_moved", …}}]}
```

* `writes` — the type the revert would record ([[What a revert writes]]); `restores` — the state it would write, in snapshot form (empty: the cell is removed).
* `filesystem` — `null`, or `{"action": "move", "from", "to"}` (paths ready for `mv`), `{"action": "restore_content"}` (from the trash), `{"action": "trash_content"}` (back to the trash). The daemon does not look whether the content is there. `requires_lock` is true when any operation has one.
* `changed_since` — `null` when the cell still holds what the operation left there; otherwise the newest logged operation that wrote it since (`{}` when that one is no longer in the log). A warning: the revert overwrites that write. A client confirms before doing so.

!! POST /revert

`{"target", "label"?, "skip_filesystem"?}` → `{"revision", "head",
"reverted_operations", "skipped_operations": [{"op_id", "reason"}]}`. `skip_filesystem`
leaves out the operations needing a file action instead of refusing — it never means
"write the metadata anyway": a revert never records a change it knows untrue of the
disk. With nothing left to apply it writes nothing (`revision: null`).

|!Status |!When |
|`400` |target off HEAD's ancestry; an `unknown` operation; the revert would leave two types under one name |
|`404` |no such revision or operation — pruned, trimmed, or never there |
|`409` |a file action needed without `skip_filesystem`; recreating a metarecord whose uuid exists again |
|`423` |a navigation lock is held |

`commit` may only narrow the target fixed at `start` (an id outside it is `400`);
operations left out are reported with `reason: "client_skipped"`. After `abort`, reversing any file action already done (or
reconciling) is the client's job, as for a rollback.
